1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,707
A fat orange carrot in the kitchen.

2
00:00:02,707 --> 00:00:05,822
A banyan tree with ropes
hanging from its branches.

3
00:00:05,822 --> 00:00:08,219
A maize plant standing on little legs.

4
00:00:08,619 --> 00:00:09,931
All three are roots.

5
00:00:09,931 --> 00:00:13,842
But none of them looks like a thin
root hiding quietly in the soil.

6
00:00:14,292 --> 00:00:18,700
Diya asked: if a root's job is to
drink water, why do some roots get fat,

7
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:20,451
and some hang from branches?

8
00:00:20,801 --> 00:00:23,229
Because a root has more than one job.

9
00:00:23,229 --> 00:00:26,618
And when the job changes,
the root changes its shape.

10
00:00:27,118 --> 00:00:31,440
By the end, you will know the three
kinds of root systems, the four regions

11
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:34,933
of a root tip, and every root
modification exams ask about.

12
00:00:35,583 --> 00:00:38,068
First, where does a root come from?

13
00:00:38,068 --> 00:00:42,023
Inside every seed sits a tiny
baby root, called the radicle.

14
00:00:42,473 --> 00:00:46,861
In mustard, the radicle keeps growing
into a main root, the primary root.

15
00:00:46,861 --> 00:00:48,759
Side roots branch out from it.

16
00:00:48,759 --> 00:00:51,025
Together they make a tap root system.

17
00:00:51,625 --> 00:00:55,075
In wheat, the primary root
lives only a short time.

18
00:00:55,075 --> 00:00:58,683
Many thin roots grow from
the base of the stem instead.

19
00:00:58,683 --> 00:01:00,763
This is a fibrous root system.

20
00:01:01,363 --> 00:01:05,834
In grass, Monstera and the banyan
tree, roots grow from parts other than

21
00:01:05,834 --> 00:01:06,771
the radicle.

22
00:01:06,771 --> 00:01:09,040
These are called adventitious roots.

23
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:14,133
Roots do four jobs: they absorb water
and minerals, hold the plant firmly,

24
00:01:14,133 --> 00:01:16,942
store food, and make
plant growth regulators.

25
00:01:17,592 --> 00:01:21,827
Now let's zoom into the tip of a
root, where all the growing happens.

26
00:01:22,177 --> 00:01:26,604
At the very end sits the root cap,
shaped like a thimble, the small cap

27
00:01:26,604 --> 00:01:28,545
a tailor wears on one finger.

28
00:01:28,545 --> 00:01:31,992
It protects the soft tip as
it pushes through the soil.

29
00:01:32,592 --> 00:01:36,183
Just above it is the region
of meristematic activity.

30
00:01:36,183 --> 00:01:41,016
Its cells are very small, with thin
walls and dense protoplasm, and they

31
00:01:41,016 --> 00:01:42,728
divide again and again.

32
00:01:43,478 --> 00:01:45,908
Above that is the region of elongation.

33
00:01:45,908 --> 00:01:50,314
Here the new cells stretch and grow
longer, and this is how the root grows

34
00:01:50,314 --> 00:01:51,214
in length.

35
00:01:51,871 --> 00:01:54,278
Then comes the region of maturation.

36
00:01:54,278 --> 00:01:59,111
The cells settle into their final jobs,
and some skin cells grow fine threads

37
00:01:59,111 --> 00:02:00,439
called root hairs.

38
00:02:01,189 --> 00:02:03,360
Root hairs are the real drinkers.

39
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:06,223
They absorb water and
minerals from the soil.

40
00:02:06,873 --> 00:02:08,354
Now the big question.

41
00:02:08,354 --> 00:02:12,844
Roots also change shape for three
other jobs: storing food, support, and

42
00:02:12,844 --> 00:02:13,744
breathing.

43
00:02:14,123 --> 00:02:15,686
Job one, storing food.

44
00:02:15,686 --> 00:02:19,405
The tap roots of carrot and
turnip swell up with stored food.

45
00:02:20,155 --> 00:02:22,276
Sweet potato stores food too.

46
00:02:22,276 --> 00:02:26,581
But its swollen roots are
adventitious roots, not a tap root.

47
00:02:27,331 --> 00:02:28,676
Job two, support.

48
00:02:28,676 --> 00:02:31,834
A banyan tree sends roots
down from its branches.

49
00:02:31,834 --> 00:02:35,899
They reach the soil and hold up
the heavy branches like pillars.

50
00:02:35,899 --> 00:02:37,400
These are prop roots.

51
00:02:38,150 --> 00:02:42,268
In maize and sugarcane, supporting
roots come out of the lower nodes of

52
00:02:42,268 --> 00:02:43,168
the stem.

53
00:02:43,022 --> 00:02:46,165
A node is a point on the
stem where a leaf grows.

54
00:02:46,165 --> 00:02:47,627
These are stilt roots.

55
00:02:48,377 --> 00:02:49,675
Job three, breathing.

56
00:02:49,675 --> 00:02:53,092
Rhizophora grows in swampy mud,
where there is very little air.

57
00:02:53,092 --> 00:02:56,485
Many of its roots come out of
the ground and grow straight up.

58
00:02:57,235 --> 00:02:58,459
These are pneumatophores.

59
00:02:58,459 --> 00:03:00,703
They help the plant get
oxygen for respiration.

60
00:03:01,453 --> 00:03:04,813
Now the pair students mix up:
prop roots and stilt roots.

61
00:03:05,163 --> 00:03:07,359
Both do the same job: support.

62
00:03:07,359 --> 00:03:11,851
And both are adventitious, because
they do not grow from the radicle.

63
00:03:12,301 --> 00:03:16,086
But prop roots come down from
branches, like in the banyan.

64
00:03:16,086 --> 00:03:20,434
Stilt roots come out of the lower
nodes of the stem, like in maize and

65
00:03:20,434 --> 00:03:21,334
sugarcane.

66
00:03:21,858 --> 00:03:22,758
Your turn.

67
00:03:22,663 --> 00:03:26,620
A sugarcane plant has roots coming
out of the bottom of its stem.

68
00:03:26,620 --> 00:03:28,470
Prop roots, or stilt roots?

69
00:03:28,470 --> 00:03:29,654
Pause and decide.

70
00:03:32,854 --> 00:03:33,754
Stilt roots!

71
00:03:33,710 --> 00:03:36,306
They grow from the
lower nodes of the stem.

72
00:03:36,306 --> 00:03:38,391
Prop roots come down from branches.

73
00:03:39,041 --> 00:03:40,056
Remember this.

74
00:03:40,056 --> 00:03:44,448
When the job changes, the root changes
shape: it can store, support or

75
00:03:44,448 --> 00:03:45,348
breathe.

76
00:03:45,721 --> 00:03:47,361
Now, a worked example.

77
00:03:47,361 --> 00:03:48,854
Let's name four roots.

78
00:03:49,204 --> 00:03:52,514
A carrot, fat and orange,
grown from the radicle.

79
00:03:52,514 --> 00:03:54,400
That is a storage tap root.

80
00:03:54,850 --> 00:03:59,023
A sweet potato, with swollen roots
that do not come from the radicle.

81
00:03:59,023 --> 00:04:01,219
That is a storage adventitious root.

82
00:04:01,669 --> 00:04:04,170
Pillars dropping from banyan branches.

83
00:04:04,170 --> 00:04:05,753
That is a prop root.

84
00:04:06,203 --> 00:04:08,734
Roots poking straight up out of swamp mud.

85
00:04:08,734 --> 00:04:10,245
That is a pneumatophore.

86
00:04:10,895 --> 00:04:12,359
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:04:12,709 --> 00:04:16,886
Many students think sweet potato is
a stem, just like potato, because the

88
00:04:16,886 --> 00:04:18,073
names sound alike.

89
00:04:18,473 --> 00:04:23,123
But sweet potato is a root, an
adventitious root that stores food.

90
00:04:23,123 --> 00:04:24,932
The potato is the stem.

91
00:04:25,582 --> 00:04:26,921
Where is this asked?

92
00:04:26,921 --> 00:04:31,089
As statements about the regions of
the root tip, or matching a plant with

93
00:04:31,089 --> 00:04:32,464
its root modification.

94
00:04:33,064 --> 00:04:34,177
Exam level now.

95
00:04:34,177 --> 00:04:36,545
Which of these statements are correct?

96
00:04:36,545 --> 00:04:37,706
Pause and solve.

97
00:04:43,106 --> 00:04:44,371
The answer is B.

98
00:04:44,371 --> 00:04:47,234
Statements two, three
and four are correct.

99
00:04:47,734 --> 00:04:49,425
Statement one is wrong.

100
00:04:49,425 --> 00:04:54,510
Root hairs grow from the region of
maturation, not the region of elongation.

101
00:04:55,010 --> 00:04:59,148
Small dividing cells near the tip,
stilt roots from the lower nodes, and

102
00:04:59,148 --> 00:05:01,570
pneumatophores for oxygen are all correct.

103
00:05:02,170 --> 00:05:03,711
Here is your recap.

104
00:05:03,711 --> 00:05:05,295
Screenshot this card.

105
00:05:08,495 --> 00:05:10,681
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

106
00:05:11,081 --> 00:05:11,981
One.

107
00:05:11,536 --> 00:05:14,292
Which region of the root
tip grows root hairs?

108
00:05:15,192 --> 00:05:16,092
Two.

109
00:05:15,599 --> 00:05:18,555
How is a carrot root different
from a sweet potato root?

110
00:05:19,455 --> 00:05:20,355
Three.

111
00:05:19,807 --> 00:05:21,726
How do pneumatophores help
Rhizophora in swampy mud?

112
00:05:22,826 --> 00:05:26,880
Next time: a potato has eyes, and
ginger grows sideways under the soil.

113
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:27,881
Are they roots?

114
00:05:27,881 --> 00:05:30,501
We find out in the stem
and its modifications.
